1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,637
August 1950. Dabu-dong,

2
00:00:02,657 --> 00:00:04,833
North Gyeongsang Province.

3
00:00:04,875 --> 00:00:06,561
From here, the city of Taegu lay

4
00:00:06,581 --> 00:00:08,917
barely twenty kilometers away.

5
00:00:09,153 --> 00:00:10,916
The North Koreans were certain.

6
00:00:10,936 --> 00:00:14,357
"Cross this pass, and Taegu is ours."

7
00:00:14,597 --> 00:00:15,881
For more than a month since the

8
00:00:15,901 --> 00:00:18,237
war began, the ROK Army had

9
00:00:18,257 --> 00:00:20,809
known only defeat and retreat.

10
00:00:20,879 --> 00:00:22,362
This time, the outcome

11
00:00:22,382 --> 00:00:24,080
seemed already decided.

12
00:00:24,325 --> 00:00:25,996
If Taegu fell, the entire

13
00:00:26,016 --> 00:00:28,295
Naktong line would collapse.

14
00:00:28,385 --> 00:00:31,596
And after that Pusan, and the sea.

15
00:00:31,833 --> 00:00:34,174
Holding this final gate were the ROK 1st

16
00:00:34,194 --> 00:00:37,462
Division and the U.S. 27th Infantry Regiment.

17
00:00:37,543 --> 00:00:39,961
They were outnumbered more than two to one.

18
00:00:40,352 --> 00:00:41,872
Yet fifty-five days later,

19
00:00:41,892 --> 00:00:44,247
it was the North Korean army that broke.

20
00:00:44,490 --> 00:00:45,878
Where hilltops changed

21
00:00:45,898 --> 00:00:47,699
hands several times a day,

22
00:00:47,719 --> 00:00:49,429
and tanks traded fire through

23
00:00:49,449 --> 00:00:51,250
the night in the valley below.

24
00:00:51,292 --> 00:00:53,583
This is the story of the last line on

25
00:00:53,603 --> 00:00:57,054
the Naktong: the Battle of Dabu-dong.

26
00:00:57,274 --> 00:01:01,828
June 25th, 1950. One month into the war,

27
00:01:01,917 --> 00:01:03,662
ROK and UN forces had been

28
00:01:03,682 --> 00:01:05,724
pushed south without pause.

29
00:01:05,957 --> 00:01:08,423
By early August, all that remained was a small

30
00:01:08,443 --> 00:01:10,790
corner in the southeast of the peninsula.

31
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:12,920
A line drawn along the Naktong

32
00:01:12,940 --> 00:01:15,099
River: the Pusan Perimeter.

33
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:17,172
To break it, the North Koreans

34
00:01:17,192 --> 00:01:19,811
drove five divisions toward Taegu.

35
00:01:19,842 --> 00:01:21,017
And the tip of that spear

36
00:01:21,037 --> 00:01:22,708
pointed at Dabu-dong.

37
00:01:22,932 --> 00:01:25,265
In early August, the ROK 1st Division

38
00:01:25,285 --> 00:01:27,484
first held the Naktong riverbank for

39
00:01:27,504 --> 00:01:30,489
nine days, blocking every crossing.

40
00:01:30,714 --> 00:01:32,436
Then, on August 12th,

41
00:01:32,456 --> 00:01:35,690
it moved to a new front: Dabu-dong.

42
00:01:35,925 --> 00:01:37,203
Why Dabu-dong?

43
00:01:37,274 --> 00:01:39,838
Because the main road from Sangju to Taegu

44
00:01:39,858 --> 00:01:42,524
funnels into this one narrow valley.

45
00:01:42,753 --> 00:01:44,780
To the west, Mount Yuhak,

46
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:47,409
eight hundred thirty-seven meters high.

47
00:01:47,429 --> 00:01:49,468
To the east, Mount Ka,

48
00:01:49,488 --> 00:01:51,266
at nine hundred and two.

49
00:01:51,287 --> 00:01:52,805
The single road between them

50
00:01:52,825 --> 00:01:55,023
was the last gate into Taegu.

51
00:01:55,250 --> 00:01:56,952
The ROK 1st Division was commanded

52
00:01:56,972 --> 00:01:59,422
by Brigadier General Paik Sun-yup,

53
00:01:59,442 --> 00:02:01,413
not yet thirty years old.

54
00:02:01,581 --> 00:02:02,905
His division drew the final

55
00:02:02,925 --> 00:02:04,931
line in front of that gate.

56
00:02:05,187 --> 00:02:07,069
Facing him were the North Korean

57
00:02:07,089 --> 00:02:10,016
13th, 1st, and 3rd Divisions.

58
00:02:10,116 --> 00:02:12,271
Tank-led units that had won battle

59
00:02:12,291 --> 00:02:14,785
after battle since the invasion began.

60
00:02:15,020 --> 00:02:17,691
The ROK 1st Division, by contrast,

61
00:02:17,711 --> 00:02:20,867
had retreated for over a month from the Imjin River,

62
00:02:20,887 --> 00:02:23,199
losing much of its men and equipment.

63
00:02:23,354 --> 00:02:24,825
Now its exhausted soldiers

64
00:02:24,845 --> 00:02:26,948
faced the heaviest task of all.

65
00:02:27,177 --> 00:02:29,984
In mid-August, the main assault began.

66
00:02:30,107 --> 00:02:31,737
The North Koreans seized the

67
00:02:31,757 --> 00:02:33,934
summit of Mount Yuhak first.

68
00:02:34,162 --> 00:02:35,872
For the ROK troops below,

69
00:02:35,892 --> 00:02:38,386
the summit was all but impregnable.

70
00:02:38,525 --> 00:02:40,462
Every climb up the rock face was

71
00:02:40,482 --> 00:02:42,920
met with a rain of grenades.

72
00:02:43,159 --> 00:02:44,403
Shelling by day.

73
00:02:44,423 --> 00:02:46,803
Hand-to-hand fighting by night.

74
00:02:46,972 --> 00:02:49,855
Survivors recall that when the ammunition ran out,

75
00:02:49,875 --> 00:02:51,987
they fought with bayonets and rocks.

76
00:02:52,233 --> 00:02:55,175
The heaviest losses fell on Hill 837.

77
00:02:55,230 --> 00:02:57,691
And Hill 328 changed hands

78
00:02:57,711 --> 00:03:00,273
fifteen times in twelve days.

79
00:03:00,499 --> 00:03:02,573
On the hilltops, water and rice

80
00:03:02,593 --> 00:03:04,849
balls rarely arrived on time.

81
00:03:04,904 --> 00:03:06,682
The ammunition and food that did make

82
00:03:06,702 --> 00:03:08,390
it up were carried on the backs of

83
00:03:08,410 --> 00:03:10,971
local villagers with wooden A-frames.

84
00:03:11,193 --> 00:03:12,412
August 18th.

85
00:03:12,471 --> 00:03:14,159
North Korean shells landed

86
00:03:14,179 --> 00:03:15,968
near Taegu Station.

87
00:03:16,219 --> 00:03:18,266
The government relocated to Pusan,

88
00:03:18,286 --> 00:03:20,792
and Taegu filled with refugees.

89
00:03:20,847 --> 00:03:22,375
It was, quite literally,

90
00:03:22,395 --> 00:03:24,064
the edge of the cliff.

91
00:03:24,301 --> 00:03:26,170
Around this time, ROK soldiers

92
00:03:26,190 --> 00:03:28,254
defending the hills began abandoning

93
00:03:28,274 --> 00:03:30,359
their positions, one by one.

94
00:03:30,428 --> 00:03:31,637
Then the division commander,

95
00:03:31,657 --> 00:03:34,653
Paik Sun-yup, stepped forward himself.

96
00:03:34,891 --> 00:03:36,441
"We have nowhere left to retreat.

97
00:03:36,461 --> 00:03:37,864
If we are pushed back here,

98
00:03:37,884 --> 00:03:39,572
we will be driven into the sea."

99
00:03:39,592 --> 00:03:42,247
"If I fall back in fear, then you shoot me."

100
00:03:42,477 --> 00:03:44,054
With their commander charging at the

101
00:03:44,074 --> 00:03:46,080
front, the men who had been falling

102
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:48,401
back surged up the hill again.

103
00:03:48,482 --> 00:03:51,238
The crumbling line held once more.

104
00:03:51,474 --> 00:03:53,498
On the night of August 21st,

105
00:03:53,518 --> 00:03:55,899
the ROK 12th Regiment launched a night

106
00:03:55,919 --> 00:03:59,158
raid and finally retook Mount Yuhak.

107
00:03:59,373 --> 00:04:01,108
Meanwhile, the road through

108
00:04:01,128 --> 00:04:03,178
the valley north of Dabu-dong.

109
00:04:03,317 --> 00:04:04,481
It was held by the U.S.

110
00:04:04,501 --> 00:04:07,407
27th Infantry Regiment, the 'Wolfhounds',

111
00:04:07,427 --> 00:04:09,619
committed on August 17th.

112
00:04:09,832 --> 00:04:12,345
Their commander was Colonel John Michaelis.

113
00:04:12,365 --> 00:04:15,042
An elite Eighth Army regiment, rushed like

114
00:04:15,062 --> 00:04:18,420
a fire brigade to every crisis on the line.

115
00:04:18,646 --> 00:04:20,766
ROK troops held the ridges on either

116
00:04:20,786 --> 00:04:22,917
side, while American tanks and

117
00:04:22,937 --> 00:04:25,272
infantry blocked the valley floor.

118
00:04:25,341 --> 00:04:27,792
At dusk, the Americans laid two belts of

119
00:04:27,812 --> 00:04:30,977
mines and trip flares across the road.

120
00:04:31,371 --> 00:04:33,693
Then came the night of August 21st.

121
00:04:33,748 --> 00:04:35,208
Out of the darkness, the roar

122
00:04:35,228 --> 00:04:37,882
of tank engines began to rise.

123
00:04:38,121 --> 00:04:40,051
The North Korean 13th Division was

124
00:04:40,071 --> 00:04:41,929
pushing down the valley, led by

125
00:04:41,949 --> 00:04:45,011
T-34 tanks and self-propelled guns.

126
00:04:45,263 --> 00:04:46,773
The American tanks held their

127
00:04:46,793 --> 00:04:48,821
fire until the last moment.

128
00:04:48,911 --> 00:04:50,997
When the enemy armor was almost on top

129
00:04:51,017 --> 00:04:53,668
of them, they opened up all at once.

130
00:04:53,894 --> 00:04:56,020
With the lead tank knocked out, the vehicles

131
00:04:56,040 --> 00:04:58,683
behind it were trapped on the narrow road.

132
00:04:58,772 --> 00:05:00,345
In an instant, the valley

133
00:05:00,365 --> 00:05:02,764
became a giant trap.

134
00:05:02,995 --> 00:05:05,035
Shells streaking low along

135
00:05:05,055 --> 00:05:06,754
the dark valley floor.

136
00:05:06,774 --> 00:05:08,792
To the men watching, it looked like

137
00:05:08,812 --> 00:05:11,645
bowling balls rolling toward the pins.

138
00:05:11,887 --> 00:05:14,237
The attacks did not end that night.

139
00:05:14,327 --> 00:05:15,776
For several more nights, the North

140
00:05:15,796 --> 00:05:18,077
Koreans came down the same valley.

141
00:05:18,324 --> 00:05:21,190
The fighting raged for nearly five hours.

142
00:05:21,312 --> 00:05:23,341
When dawn broke, only burned-out

143
00:05:23,361 --> 00:05:25,269
tanks remained in the valley.

144
00:05:25,492 --> 00:05:28,064
From August 18th to 25th, U.S.

145
00:05:28,084 --> 00:05:30,749
records list North Korean losses as

146
00:05:30,769 --> 00:05:33,948
thirteen T-34 tanks, six self-propelled

147
00:05:33,968 --> 00:05:36,336
guns, and twenty-three trucks.

148
00:05:36,552 --> 00:05:38,006
In the same period, American

149
00:05:38,026 --> 00:05:39,804
losses were eight killed.

150
00:05:39,914 --> 00:05:42,056
But the ROK troops holding the ridges

151
00:05:42,076 --> 00:05:44,174
on both sides lost an estimated

152
00:05:44,194 --> 00:05:46,340
two thousand three hundred men.

153
00:05:46,593 --> 00:05:49,242
The North Korean 13th Division lost some

154
00:05:49,262 --> 00:05:51,563
three thousand men in this fight alone,

155
00:05:51,583 --> 00:05:54,532
and with them, its ability to attack.

156
00:05:54,780 --> 00:05:57,165
Around then, the division's artillery

157
00:05:57,185 --> 00:05:58,895
commander, Lieutenant Colonel Jeong

158
00:05:58,915 --> 00:06:02,263
Bong-uk, defected to the ROK lines.

159
00:06:02,352 --> 00:06:04,382
A sign that the North Korean army

160
00:06:04,402 --> 00:06:06,773
was beginning to crack from within.

161
00:06:07,016 --> 00:06:08,510
So how did Dabu-dong hold

162
00:06:08,530 --> 00:06:10,023
for fifty-five days?

163
00:06:10,099 --> 00:06:12,182
Let's break it down into four factors.

164
00:06:12,407 --> 00:06:14,196
First, terrain.

165
00:06:14,429 --> 00:06:16,434
The narrow valley between Mount Yuhak

166
00:06:16,454 --> 00:06:19,005
and Mount Ka was a chokepoint no large

167
00:06:19,025 --> 00:06:21,463
army could push through all at once.

168
00:06:21,593 --> 00:06:23,587
In this gorge, the advantage

169
00:06:23,607 --> 00:06:26,094
of numbers simply disappeared.

170
00:06:26,325 --> 00:06:30,504
Second, combined ROK-U.S. operations.

171
00:06:30,750 --> 00:06:33,068
The ROK held the ridgelines; the

172
00:06:33,088 --> 00:06:35,229
Americans held the valley road.

173
00:06:35,398 --> 00:06:37,859
Infantry, armor, and artillery moved as

174
00:06:37,879 --> 00:06:40,393
a single, interlocking line of defense.

175
00:06:40,635 --> 00:06:42,346
In this battle, Paik found himself

176
00:06:42,366 --> 00:06:44,111
directing the American 27th

177
00:06:44,131 --> 00:06:45,955
Regiment at the front as well.

178
00:06:46,025 --> 00:06:48,168
A rare moment: a Korean general

179
00:06:48,188 --> 00:06:50,275
in command of a U.S. regiment.

180
00:06:50,494 --> 00:06:52,401
Third, firepower.

181
00:06:52,646 --> 00:06:55,490
On August 16th, ninety-eight B-29

182
00:06:55,510 --> 00:06:57,242
bombers dropped some nine hundred

183
00:06:57,262 --> 00:07:00,030
sixty tons of bombs around Waegwan.

184
00:07:00,160 --> 00:07:02,199
In the Bowling Alley fight, a single U.S.

185
00:07:02,219 --> 00:07:04,533
artillery battery fired sixteen hundred

186
00:07:04,553 --> 00:07:07,692
and sixty-one 105-millimeter rounds.

187
00:07:07,938 --> 00:07:10,743
Fourth, the resolve of command.

188
00:07:10,979 --> 00:07:12,717
A unit whose general stands at the

189
00:07:12,737 --> 00:07:14,890
very front does not break easily.

190
00:07:14,950 --> 00:07:17,127
And that resolve built trust between

191
00:07:17,147 --> 00:07:19,357
Korean and American troops fighting

192
00:07:19,377 --> 00:07:21,807
side by side for the first time.

193
00:07:22,039 --> 00:07:23,983
According to South Korean records,

194
00:07:24,003 --> 00:07:26,168
friendly casualties in this period

195
00:07:26,188 --> 00:07:28,387
numbered around ten thousand.

196
00:07:28,552 --> 00:07:30,980
North Korean casualties reached some

197
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,360
seventeen thousand five hundred.

198
00:07:33,596 --> 00:07:35,210
Fifty-five desperate days that

199
00:07:35,230 --> 00:07:37,291
numbers alone cannot capture.

200
00:07:37,524 --> 00:07:40,180
While Dabu-dong held, the United Nations

201
00:07:40,200 --> 00:07:42,194
was preparing its counterstroke.

202
00:07:42,363 --> 00:07:46,511
September 15th, 1950: the Inchon landing.

203
00:07:46,756 --> 00:07:49,704
The next day, ROK and UN forces along the

204
00:07:49,724 --> 00:07:52,765
Naktong launched a general counteroffensive.

205
00:07:52,990 --> 00:07:54,620
Dabu-dong became the starting

206
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:56,129
line of the breakout.

207
00:07:56,353 --> 00:07:59,185
Today, a war memorial stands at Dabu-dong,

208
00:07:59,205 --> 00:08:01,688
honoring that sacrifice every year.

209
00:08:01,902 --> 00:08:04,422
Even now, the remains of Korean War dead

210
00:08:04,442 --> 00:08:07,443
are still being recovered on Mount Yuhak.

211
00:08:07,659 --> 00:08:09,938
Young men who fell on these ridges,

212
00:08:09,958 --> 00:08:12,191
many without even leaving a name.

213
00:08:12,417 --> 00:08:14,154
Most of them were barely twenty.

214
00:08:14,383 --> 00:08:15,833
And among those who fought on this

215
00:08:15,853 --> 00:08:17,073
mountain were villagers who

216
00:08:17,093 --> 00:08:19,585
carried A-frames instead of rifles.

217
00:08:19,977 --> 00:08:22,925
The last twenty kilometers to Taegu.

218
00:08:22,972 --> 00:08:25,199
In the end, what held that road was

219
00:08:25,219 --> 00:08:27,257
people who refused to step back.

220
00:08:27,484 --> 00:08:29,323
If this story meant something to you,

221
00:08:29,343 --> 00:08:31,792
please subscribe and like the video.

222
00:08:31,881 --> 00:08:33,044
We'll continue to bring the

223
00:08:33,064 --> 00:08:34,858
forgotten records of war to light.
